---

<p>A SQL type determines which values a column can hold and what operations mean for them. The shop’s product code <code>P7</code>, price <code>20.00</code> and order time may all arrive from an application as text. They describe different things: an identity, an amount and a moment. Treating them alike loses distinctions the application needs.</p>

<p>The choice shows up later in a calculation or comparison. Dividing integers can discard a fraction before it reaches a decimal column. Two different clock readings can be the same moment. Two strings can compare equal under one rule and different under another. And when a value is missing, a test need not be either true or false.</p>

<p>Every example starts from the small tea shop introduced in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/">Relational databases</a>. P7 is a blue mug priced at 20.00. Order 103 was placed at 10:15 UTC on 20 October 2026. The SQL and engine-specific rules below are PostgreSQL 18’s; the questions about meaning apply wherever the data is stored.</p>

<h2 id="types">Types, values and their written forms</h2>

<p>A written form is how a value enters or leaves the database. It is not necessarily the value’s identity. The first drawing puts three of the shop’s values beside the operations each is meant to support.</p>

<Figure number={1} title="Three strings arriving at the shop">
  <div class="value-cards">
    <div class="value-card"><span class="value-label">Product code · text</span><strong class="value-big">P7</strong><p>Find this product.</p><div class="value-result">Does the code match?</div></div>
    <div class="value-card"><span class="value-label">Price · numeric(8,2)</span><strong class="value-big">20.00</strong><p>One mug’s current price.</p><div class="value-result">2 × 20.00 = 40.00</div></div>
    <div class="value-card"><span class="value-label">Placed at · timestamptz</span><strong>20 Oct 2026<br />10:15 UTC</strong><p>Order 103 was placed then.</p><div class="value-result">Before or after another order?</div></div>
  </div>
  <p class="value-rail">The type supplies operations. The shop must still supply the meaning: which code policy, which monetary unit, and which event the time records.</p>
  <p slot="caption">A code is compared, a price is used in arithmetic, and an order time locates an event. Receiving all three as strings does not make text the right storage type for all three.</p>
</Figure>

<p>In SQL, <code>'20.00'</code> is a quoted string literal whose eventual type can be supplied by context. <code>numeric '20.00'</code> gives it an explicit type. A <em>cast</em> converts a value to another type: PostgreSQL’s <code>'20.00'::numeric</code> is the same request as <code>CAST('20.00' AS numeric)</code>. Conversion may reject input, round it or discard information; it is more than changing a label.</p>

<p>The type also leaves business rules open. An integer permits negative counts, and a decimal does not say which currency an amount uses. The shop’s prices have no assigned currency in this example. A real application must record or otherwise fix the monetary unit before adding amounts together. Constraints add rules such as a non-negative stock count; a type alone cannot express every valid fact.</p>

<h2 id="numbers">Numbers, money and rounding</h2>

<p>Counts such as stock and order quantity are whole numbers, so an integer fits. For amounts that must add up exactly in decimal, use an exact decimal type such as <code>numeric</code>, or an integer with an explicitly named unit such as hundredths of the monetary unit. Floating-point types such as <code>double precision</code> store approximations. They suit measurements and calculations whose allowed error permits that approximation.</p>

<p>The distinction is in the arithmetic, even when the display hides it. In PostgreSQL, <code>0.1::numeric + 0.2::numeric = 0.3::numeric</code> is true. The corresponding <code>double precision</code> comparison is false. Rounding the displayed answer to one decimal place can make both look like <code>0.3</code>; it does not make the underlying values equal.</p>

<h3 id="conversion">Conversion can change the answer</h3>

<p>Suppose we divide P7’s amount of 20 into three shares. Start with integer inputs, then move the conversion from after the division to before it.</p>

<Figure number={2} title="A cast before or after division">
  <div class="value-branch">
    <div class="value-path"><span class="value-label">Convert after dividing</span><p><code>20 / 3</code> → <strong>6</strong></p><p>Integer division discards the fraction.</p><div class="value-result"><code>(20 / 3)::numeric</code><strong class="value-big">6</strong></div></div>
    <div class="value-path"><span class="value-label">Convert before dividing</span><p><code>20::numeric / 3</code></p><p>Division uses decimal arithmetic.</p><div class="value-result"><strong class="value-big">6.6666…</strong><span class="value-detail">PostgreSQL returns a finite decimal approximation of this recurring fraction.</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <p class="value-rail">Changing the result’s type cannot recover the fraction the first calculation discarded.</p>
  <p slot="caption">Both expressions end with a numeric result. Only the second gives division decimal inputs. The arrows trace the meaning of the expressions, not separate database statements.</p>
</Figure>

<Sql label="Compare division and conversion" code={`SELECT (20 / 3)::numeric AS converted_after,
       20::numeric / 3   AS converted_before;
-- converted_after: 6
-- converted_before: 6.6666666666666667`} />

<p>PostgreSQL can convert inputs implicitly too: <code>20 / 3.0</code> uses numeric division because the decimal literal changes the expression’s types. Make the conversion explicit, or supply correctly typed parameters, when the calculation depends on it. The same problem crosses the application boundary: converting an approximate number to decimal cannot reconstruct an original exact amount that was already lost.</p>

<h3 id="precision">Precision and scale</h3>

<p>The shop declares prices as <code>numeric(8,2)</code>. The precision, 8, allows eight decimal digits in all; the scale, 2, keeps two after the decimal point. For finite values that leaves six before it, from −999999.99 to 999999.99. The price’s separate <code>CHECK (price &gt;= 0)</code> rules out negative amounts.</p>

<p>PostgreSQL first rounds a value to the column’s scale, then checks whether it fits. <code>20.005::numeric(8,2)</code> becomes <code>20.01</code>. <code>999999.995::numeric(8,2)</code> rounds beyond the available six digits and fails. Formatting <code>20</code> as <code>20.00</code> for a screen is a different operation: it need not change the stored value.</p>

<p>Exact decimal storage still leaves a rounding decision. There is no two-decimal amount equal to one third of 20.00. The next drawing compares rounding every share independently with an allocation that preserves the total.</p>

<Figure number={3} title="Three rounded shares of 20.00">
  <div class="value-rule"><span class="value-label">Starting amount</span><strong class="value-big">20.00 ÷ 3</strong></div>
  <div class="value-branch">
    <div class="value-path"><span class="value-label">Round every share to two places</span><p><strong>6.67 + 6.67 + 6.67</strong></p><div class="value-result"><strong class="value-big">20.01</strong>One hundredth too much.</div></div>
    <div class="value-path"><span class="value-label">Allocate the remainder deliberately</span><p><strong>6.67 + 6.67 + 6.66</strong></p><div class="value-result"><strong class="value-big">20.00</strong>The total is preserved.</div></div>
  </div>
  <p slot="caption">An invented allocation of P7’s amount, with no payment added to the shop. Choosing who receives the smaller share is a business rule; decimal storage does not choose it.</p>
</Figure>

<p>Decide where rounding belongs: per item, per tax line, per order, or when an amount is settled. Different points can give different totals. Storing 20.00 as 2,000 hundredths avoids decimal storage rounding, but dividing 2,000 by 3 still leaves a remainder. Scaled integers need a named unit, enough range for intermediate calculations and a division policy just as decimals do.</p>

<h2 id="time">Dates, local times and instants</h2>

<p>A calendar date, a local clock reading and a moment are different facts. Use <code>date</code> for a day such as a product’s best-before date. Use <code>timestamp without time zone</code> for local date-and-time fields whose meaning does not yet identify an instant. Use <code>timestamptz</code>, PostgreSQL’s short name for <code>timestamp with time zone</code>, for the instant an order was placed.</p>

<p>Order 103’s placement time shows why a moment is different from its written clock reading.</p>

<Figure number={4} title="Two clock readings, one order time">
  <div class="value-branch">
    <div class="value-card"><span class="value-label">Input at UTC</span><strong>20 October 2026</strong><strong class="value-big">10:15 +00</strong></div>
    <div class="value-card"><span class="value-label">Input two hours ahead</span><strong>20 October 2026</strong><strong class="value-big">12:15 +02</strong></div>
  </div>
  <div class="value-rule"><span class="value-label">Both identify the same instant</span><strong>Order 103 · 20 October 2026, 10:15 UTC</strong></div>
  <div class="value-branch">
    <div class="value-path"><span class="value-label">Display in UTC</span><strong>10:15 +00</strong></div>
    <div class="value-path"><span class="value-label">Display in Europe/Stockholm</span><strong>12:15 +02</strong></div>
  </div>
  <p slot="caption">Changing the display zone changes the clock reading. It does not move the order. PostgreSQL retains the instant, not the input’s zone name or its original offset.</p>
</Figure>

<Sql label="Compare two representations of order 103's instant" code={`SELECT timestamptz '2026-10-20 10:15+00'
     = timestamptz '2026-10-20 12:15+02' AS same_instant;
-- true`} />

<p>An explicit offset says how a clock reading relates to UTC. If a <code>timestamptz</code> input has none, PostgreSQL interprets it in the session’s <code>TimeZone</code>. Output uses that zone too. A bare <code>timestamp</code> means <code>timestamp without time zone</code>; adding <code>+02</code> inside its literal does not turn it into an instant. PostgreSQL ignores that offset for this type.</p>

<h3 id="zones">Time zones and calendar arithmetic</h3>

<p>A named zone such as <code>Europe/Stockholm</code> supplies rules for converting local clocks on particular dates. A fixed offset such as <code>+02</code> does not describe those rules. When clocks move back, a local time can occur twice; when they move forward, some local times never occur. A scheduling system needs a policy to resolve such cases. PostgreSQL has its own documented resolution rules, so accepting an ambiguous input does not establish that it meant what the user intended.</p>

<p>Calendar arithmetic also needs a zone. On 25 October 2026 the Stockholm clock moves back an hour. Compare the next midnight with 24 elapsed hours after this midnight.</p>

<Figure number={5} title="A calendar day with 25 elapsed hours">
  <div class="value-rule"><span class="value-label">Start · Europe/Stockholm</span><strong>25 October, 00:00 +02</strong></div>
  <div class="value-branch">
    <div class="value-path"><span class="value-label">Add 1 calendar day in this zone</span><p>Pass through the repeated hour.</p><strong>26 October, 00:00 +01</strong><div class="value-result"><strong class="value-big">25 hours</strong>have elapsed.</div></div>
    <div class="value-path"><span class="value-label">Add 24 elapsed hours</span><p>Stop an hour before midnight.</p><strong>25 October, 23:00 +01</strong><div class="value-result"><strong class="value-big">24 hours</strong>have elapsed.</div></div>
  </div>
  <p slot="caption">A separate clock example, not another shop order. The zone and starting date determine this difference. On an ordinary day these two additions reach the same clock reading.</p>
</Figure>

<Sql label="Calendar and elapsed additions in Stockholm" code={`SET TIME ZONE 'Europe/Stockholm';
SELECT timestamptz '2026-10-25 00:00+02' + interval '1 day'
         AS next_calendar_day,
       timestamptz '2026-10-25 00:00+02' + interval '24 hours'
         AS after_24_hours;
-- next_calendar_day: 2026-10-26 00:00:00+01
-- after_24_hours:    2026-10-25 23:00:00+01`} />

<p>Deriving a business date from an instant is another zone choice. In PostgreSQL, <code>placed_at::date</code> uses the session zone; converting a local timestamp to <code>timestamptz</code>, or comparing the two types, can depend on it too. State the intended zone in the expression:</p>

<Sql label="Derive order dates in an explicit business zone" code={`SELECT order_id,
       (placed_at AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Stockholm')::date
         AS business_date
FROM orders;
-- Order 103: 2026-10-20`} />

<p><code>AT TIME ZONE</code> here produces the local date-and-time fields for that instant in Stockholm; the cast then takes their date. The zone is a choice for this example, not a property of the shop’s order row. If the original zone matters as a fact, store it separately.</p>

<h2 id="text">Text, collation and equality</h2>

<p>Text preserves characters. A <em>collation</em> supplies rules used to compare and order those strings. The name shown to a customer and the rule used to find it need not be the same representation: you can preserve a name’s spelling while searching in a way that ignores selected differences.</p>

<p>The following pairs make four separate choices visible. The code pairs are candidate inputs to compare, not extra products in the shop.</p>

<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Differences a text comparison policy may preserve or ignore">
<table><caption>Which text differences should count?</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Pair</th><th scope="col">Difference</th><th scope="col">Decision to make</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>P7</code> / <code>p7</code></td><td>Case</td><td>Are product codes case-sensitive?</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>cafe</code> / <code>café</code></td><td>Accent</td><td>May search ignore an accent? May an identifier?</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>é</code> / <code>e + ◌́</code></td><td>One code point / e followed by a combining accent</td><td>Should different Unicode normal forms match?</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>P7</code> / <code>P7␠</code></td><td>Trailing space (␠)</td><td>Reject it, trim it, or preserve it as significant?</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>

<p>In PostgreSQL, a deterministic collation breaks comparison ties using the bytes, so different byte sequences remain unequal. A deliberately configured nondeterministic collation can make selected differences insignificant. Which ones depends on its provider and settings: choosing a locale does not by itself mean case-insensitive equality. Such a rule can affect a unique key as well as a search.</p>

<p>For this shop, suppose product codes must match their stored spelling exactly. A deterministic comparison keeps <code>P7</code> and <code>p7</code> distinct. If the input policy instead accepts lower-case ASCII codes, canonicalise them consistently before storing and looking them up, then enforce uniqueness under the same policy. Applying <code>lower()</code> somewhere in a query is not a complete policy for every language, accent or Unicode normal form.</p>

<p>Ordering is a policy too. PostgreSQL’s <code>COLLATE "C"</code> orders text bytewise: among ASCII strings, <code>P10</code> sorts before <code>P7</code> because <code>1</code> precedes <code>7</code>. It does not interpret the suffixes as quantities. A person’s alphabetical list may need a locale-aware order; a machine code may need a different one.</p>

<p>Use a length limit when the domain supplies one, such as a protocol’s maximum code length. PostgreSQL’s <code>text</code> has no declared length limit, and <code>varchar(n)</code> limits characters, not bytes. <code>char(n)</code> pads with spaces and disregards trailing spaces in comparisons; <code>text</code> and <code>varchar</code> treat those spaces as significant under deterministic comparison. Choosing a fixed-length type for a varying name can therefore change equality as well as storage.</p>

<h2 id="identifiers">Identifiers are values with a purpose</h2>

<p>A product code identifies a product; it is not an amount. A code made only of digits can still require text if leading zeroes matter. Converting <code>'007'</code> to an integer gives 7 and loses that distinction. The shop’s <code>customer_id</code>, by contrast, is an integer used to reference a customer. Adding two customer IDs produces no useful customer fact.</p>

<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Choosing an identifier representation">
<table><caption>Choose an identifier from where it comes from</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Representation</th><th scope="col">When it fits</th><th scope="col">What it leaves to decide</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Integer</td><td>A database allocates row identities.</td><td>Generation range and a uniqueness constraint.</td></tr>
<tr><td>UUID</td><td>Several producers need to allocate identifiers without sharing a sequence.</td><td>A suitable generation algorithm and uniqueness enforcement.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Text</td><td>A code comes from a business or an external system.</td><td>Permitted spelling, normalisation and comparison rules.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>

<p>PostgreSQL’s <code>uuid</code> type stores a 128-bit identifier and accepts UUIDs generated inside or outside the database. An identity column generates integers from a sequence. Neither declaring a UUID type nor declaring <code>GENERATED ... AS IDENTITY</code> replaces a <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> or <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint.</p>

<p>Generation and meaning are separate. Two IDs can belong to duplicate customer records; detecting that needs a business definition of “the same customer”. An increasing ID is not a promise about event or commit order. A difficult-to-guess ID is not permission to read the row: the application must still authorise access. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/modelling/">Relational data modelling</a> develops which facts and keys identify a row.</p>

<h2 id="parts">When one value has parts</h2>

<p>The label <code>Green tea, 50 g</code> has punctuation and a number. That alone is no reason to split it. The reason appears when the shop wants to compare pack sizes, convert units or change the name without changing the pack quantity.</p>

<Figure number={6} title="A label assembled from values">
  <div class="value-rule"><span class="value-label">Existing product label · P3</span><strong>Green tea, 50 g</strong></div>
  <div class="value-cards" style="margin-top:.8rem">
    <div class="value-card"><span class="value-label">Product name</span><strong>Green tea</strong><p>Words shown to a customer.</p></div>
    <div class="value-card"><span class="value-label">Pack amount</span><strong class="value-big">50</strong><p>A quantity to compare.</p></div>
    <div class="value-card"><span class="value-label">Pack unit</span><strong class="value-big">g</strong><p>What the quantity measures.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="value-rail">These values can produce the same label. They also let a query ask for a 50 g pack without parsing its name.</p>
  <p slot="caption">A possible representation of the facts already present in P3’s label. The 50 g of tea is distinct from the 65 g package weight used below.</p>
</Figure>

<p>SQL can hold structured values: arrays, composite values and JSON are all available in PostgreSQL. One column need not be one indivisible string. A pack size could be represented by separate columns or by a structured value, depending on the operations and validation it needs.</p>

<p>A JSON value such as <code>{'{"amount":50,"unit":"g"}'}</code> has parts, but valid JSON syntax does not establish a valid pack size. <code>{'{"amount":"large","unit":"perhaps"}'}</code> is valid JSON too. You still need rules for the amount’s type, permitted units and missing members. Choose the representation from what must be compared, validated and updated together. Independently repeated facts and relationships raise the modelling questions developed in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/modelling/">Relational data modelling</a>.</p>

<h2 id="null">NULL and missing information</h2>

<p>Now add a package weight for each product, in grams. P3 is 65 g and P9 is 820 g. P7 has not yet been measured, so its <code>package_weight_g</code> is <code>NULL</code>. These invented measurements form a separate scenario; they do not change the shop’s prices or orders.</p>

<p><code>NULL</code> marks absent information. It is distinct from the known weight zero, from an empty string, and from having no product row at all. Here it has one precise meaning: not yet measured. If the application also needs to distinguish “not applicable” or “measurement failed”, represent those reasons explicitly, for example with a measurement status. A single NULL cannot tell you which reason applied.</p>

<h3 id="predicates">True, false and unknown</h3>

<p>A <em>predicate</em> is a test whose result can select a row. <code>package_weight_g &gt;= 100</code> is false for P3 and true for P9. For P7 the weight is absent, so the result is <em>unknown</em>, represented by a null Boolean. It might pass when measured, or it might not.</p>

<p>The figure follows those results into <code>WHERE</code>, which keeps only true. Compare the first question with <em>NOT at least 100 g</em>: P7’s unknown measurement remains unknown.</p>

<WeightFilter />

<p>The first filter keeps P9. Negating it keeps P3. P7 appears in neither result: <code>NOT unknown</code> is still unknown. So “the rows that pass” and “the rows that pass the negated test” need not divide all the input rows between them.</p>

<p><code>AND</code> and <code>OR</code> follow the same reasoning. False AND unknown is false because one failed condition is enough to rule out both being true. True OR unknown is true because one satisfied condition is enough. When neither known result settles the answer, it stays unknown.</p>

<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Combining unknown with logical operators">
<table><caption>The cases involving unknown</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">First result</th><th scope="col">AND unknown</th><th scope="col">OR unknown</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">true</th><td>unknown</td><td>true</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">false</th><td>false</td><td>unknown</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">unknown</th><td>unknown</td><td>unknown</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>

<p>Choose <em>At least 100 g OR P7</em> in the figure. P7 now passes on its code alone: unknown OR true is true. Choose <em>Not yet measured</em> to ask about absence directly. <code>IS NULL</code> returns true for P7 and false for the others; <code>IS NOT NULL</code> does the reverse.</p>

<p>The same logic explains a surprising <code>NOT IN</code>. Test the known weight 65 against a list containing 820 and NULL:</p>

<Sql label="A NULL inside an IN list" code={`SELECT 65 IN (820, NULL)     AS is_in,
       65 NOT IN (820, NULL) AS is_not_in;
-- is_in: NULL (unknown)
-- is_not_in: NULL (unknown)`} />

<p><code>IN</code> combines equality tests with OR: 65 equals 820 is false, and 65 equals NULL is unknown. False OR unknown gives unknown. <code>NOT IN</code> negates that result, leaving it unknown too. A filter using either expression keeps no row. By contrast, 65 IN (65, NULL) is true because one equality succeeds. Decide what a null list member means before using the list as an exclusion rule.</p>

<h3 id="missing-comparison">Comparing and replacing missing values</h3>

<p>Ordinary equality cannot establish that two absent measurements match. <code>NULL::integer = NULL::integer</code> is unknown, just as comparing a known weight with an absent one is unknown. Do not write <code>weight = NULL</code> to find missing values; use <code>weight IS NULL</code>.</p>

<p>Sometimes the question is whether two fields contain the same recorded information, including both being absent. <code>IS NOT DISTINCT FROM</code> expresses that comparison. It is true for two equal known values or two NULLs, and false when just one is NULL. It does not establish that two unmeasured objects have the same actual weight.</p>

<Sql label="Compare recorded absence explicitly" code={`SELECT NULL::integer = NULL::integer AS ordinary_equality,
       NULL::integer IS NOT DISTINCT FROM NULL::integer
         AS same_recorded_information;
-- ordinary_equality: NULL (unknown)
-- same_recorded_information: true`} />

<p><code>COALESCE</code> returns its first non-null argument. That is useful for a display label:</p>

<Sql label="Label a missing weight without inventing a measurement" code={`SELECT product_id,
       COALESCE(package_weight_g::text, 'Not yet measured')
         AS weight_label
FROM products;
-- P3: 65     P7: Not yet measured     P9: 820`} />

<p><code>COALESCE(package_weight_g, 0)</code> would instead supply a numeric zero. Used in a shipping calculation, it would turn an unmeasured mug into a weightless mug. Choose a fallback only when it has the intended meaning. Likewise, order 103’s lack of payment rows is a different absence from this missing attribute. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-results/">Joins and the meaning of a result row</a> follows missing matches into query results.</p>

<h3 id="operation">Equality depends on the operation</h3>

<p>The shop can also assign optional loyalty codes. Mina has <code>TEA-7</code>; Tariq and Edda have no assigned code. PostgreSQL’s default <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint permits both NULLs while refusing a second <code>TEA-7</code>. Ask for <code>NULLS NOT DISTINCT</code> and the second absent code conflicts too.</p>

<Figure number={8} title="One assigned code, two absent codes">
  <div class="value-cards">
    <div class="value-card"><span class="value-label">Mina · customer 1</span><strong class="value-big">TEA-7</strong></div>
    <div class="value-card"><span class="value-label">Tariq · customer 2</span><strong class="value-big">NULL</strong><p>No code assigned.</p></div>
    <div class="value-card"><span class="value-label">Edda · customer 3</span><strong class="value-big">NULL</strong><p>No code assigned.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="value-branch">
    <div class="value-path"><span class="value-label">UNIQUE (loyalty_code)</span><strong>All three rows allowed.</strong><p>Absent codes do not conflict.</p></div>
    <div class="value-path"><span class="value-label">UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT (loyalty_code)</span><strong>Second NULL refused.</strong><p>At most one customer may lack a code.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p slot="caption">Alternatives under PostgreSQL 18, for a single nullable code column. The second rule is usually the wrong one for an optional loyalty code: many customers may have none.</p>
</Figure>

<p>Neither rule changes what <code>NULL = NULL</code> means in a predicate. Duplicate elimination has its own rule too: <code>SELECT DISTINCT loyalty_code</code> returns one NULL for the two absent codes. The operation determines how absence participates, so do not infer uniqueness behaviour from an equality test. Defaults differ between database engines.</p>

<p><code>CHECK</code> makes another distinction. PostgreSQL accepts a row when the check is true <em>or unknown</em>. <code>CHECK (package_weight_g &gt; 0)</code> rejects a measured zero but allows the unmeasured mug. Add <code>NOT NULL</code> if every row must have a measurement. That would deliberately reject P7’s current scenario state.</p>

<p>Finally, sorting places NULLs rather than comparing their missing contents. Write <code>ORDER BY package_weight_g ASC NULLS LAST, product_id</code> to put P3, then P9, then the unmeasured P7. PostgreSQL’s ascending default puts NULLs last and descending puts them first; stating the placement makes the intended report clearer.</p>

<p>The useful question is always what distinction the application needs to preserve. A missing measurement, an absent loyalty code and an order with no payment are three different facts. Give each an explicit meaning, choose the type and comparison rule that preserve it, and carry that meaning into calculations and filters.</p>

